\[\begin{array}[]{c}W_{h}=\{\mathbf{w}\!\in\!\mathcal{C}^{0}(\bar{\Omega})^{2}; \mathbf{w}|_{\kappa}\!\in\!\mathcal{P}_{1}(\kappa)\quad\forall\kappa\!\in\! \mathcal{T}_{h}\},\\ \mathcal{P}_{1}(\kappa)=[P_{1}\oplus\operatorname{span}(\lambda_{1}\lambda_{2 }\lambda_{3})]^{2},\\ Q_{h}=\{q\!\in\!\mathcal{C}^{0}(\bar{\Omega});q|_{\kappa}\!\in\!P_{1}\quad \forall\kappa\!\in\!\mathcal{T}_{h}\}\end{array}\]

has a unique branch of solutions: \(\{(\dot{\lambda},(\mathbf{u}_{h}(\dot{\lambda}),\dot{\lambda}p_{h}(\dot{ \lambda})));\dot{\lambda}\!\in\!\Lambda\}\) and there exists a constant \(C\) independent of \(\dot{\lambda}\) such that:

\[|\mathbf{u}(\dot{\lambda})-\mathbf{u}_{h}(\dot{\lambda})|_{1,\Omega}+\left\| p(\dot{\lambda})-p_{h}(\dot{\lambda})\right\|_{0,\Omega}\leqslant Ch\{| \mathbf{u}(\dot{\lambda})|_{2,\Omega}+|p(\dot{\lambda})|_{1,\Omega}\}.\]

When \(\Omega\) is convex we have the \(L^{2}\)-estimate:

\[\left\|\mathbf{u}(\dot{\lambda})-\mathbf{u}_{h}(\dot{\lambda})\right\|_{0, \Omega}\leqslant Ch^{2}\{|\mathbf{u}(\dot{\lambda})|_{2,\Omega}+|p(\dot{ \lambda})|_{1,\Omega}\}.\]

Likewise the error estimates of Theorems II.4.3 and II.4.4 for the two "Hood-Taylor" elements carry over to the scheme (4.2) when \(\mathcal{T}_{h}\) and the branch of nonsingular solutions of the Navier-Stokes equations have the adequate regularity.

Unfortunately, the "Glowinski-Pironneau" scheme does not fit so neatly into the preceding framework and has to be analyzed separately. Recall that the velocity and pressure spaces are those of the "Hood-Taylor" scheme:

\[\begin{array}[]{c}X_{h}=\{\mathbf{v}\!\in\!\mathcal{C}^{0}(\bar{\Omega})^{2}; \mathbf{v}|_{\kappa}\!\in\!P_{2}^{2}\quad\forall\kappa\!\in\!\mathcal{T}_{h}, \mathbf{v}|_{F}=0\},\\ Q_{h}=\{q\!\in\!\mathcal{C}^{0}(\bar{\Omega});q|_{\kappa}\!\in\!P_{1}\quad \forall\kappa\!\in\!\mathcal{T}_{h}\},\end{array}\]

with the additional space

\[\Phi_{h}=Q_{h}\cap H_{0}^{1}(\Omega)\]

for the auxiliary potential. When adapted to the Navier-Stokes problem, the "Glowinski-Pironneau" method, in the version given by Remark II.4.5, reads:

(4.15) \[\begin{array}[]{c}\mbox{\it Find a triple}\ (\mathbf{u}_{h},p_{h},\mu_{h})\!\in\!X_{h} \times Q_{h}\times\Phi_{h}\,\mbox{\it satisfying:}\\ \left\{\begin{array}[]{c}v(\mathbf{grad}\,\mathbf{u}_{h},\mathbf{grad}\, \mathbf{v}_{h})+\left(\sum\limits_{j=1}^{2}(u_{h})_{j}\partial\mathbf{u}_{h}/ \partial x_{j},\mathbf{v}_{h}-\mathbf{grad}\,q_{h}\right)\\ +(\mathbf{grad}\,p_{h},\mathbf{v}_{h}-\mathbf{grad}\,q_{h})=(\mathbf{f}, \mathbf{v}_{h}-\mathbf{grad}\,q_{h})\quad\quad\forall(\mathbf{v}_{h},q_{h}) \!\in\!X_{h}\times\Phi_{h}\\ (\mathbf{u}_{h}-\mathbf{grad}\,\mu_{h},\mathbf{grad}\,q_{h})=0\quad\quad \forall q_{h}\!\in\!Q_{h}.\end{array}\right.\end{array}\]

